Subject: Full-Stack Development of a Multiplayer Card Game (Dhumbal)

Objective: Develop a full-stack, real-time multiplayer web application for the Nepalese card game "Dhumbal" (also known as Jhyap). The game should allow users to create/join rooms and play against 2-5 players.

1. Tech Stack Requirements:

Frontend: Next.js with Tailwind CSS for styling. Use Framer Motion for card animations.

Backend: Node.js with Express.

Real-time Communication: Socket.io for game state synchronization.

State Management: Use React Context or Zustand for frontend state.

2. Game Logic & Rules (Dhumbal):

Deck: Standard 52-card deck.

Deal: 5 cards to each player.

Card Values: Ace = 1, 2-10 = Face value, J = 11, Q = 12, K = 13.

Objective: Have the lowest total hand value.

Dropping Cards: - A player can drop a single card, a set (pairs, triples, quads of the same rank), or a sequence (3+ cards of the same suit).

Picking Cards: - After dropping, a player must pick a card from the deck or one of the last card thrown by the previous player.

Declaring (Show/Dhumbal): - A player can declare if their total hand value is 5 or less.

If the declarer has the lowest points, they get 0 points for the round.

Penalty: If another player has a total equal to or lower than the declarer, the declarer gets a penalty of +25 points plus their current hand value.

Winning: The game continues in rounds until a player reaches a target score (e.g., 100). The player with the lowest total score wins.

3. Feature Requirements:

Lobby System: Users can enter a name and create a room with a unique Code, or join an existing room.

Game State Sync: The server must manage the deck, the "throw" pile, turn order, and player hands to prevent cheating.

Turn Timer: 30-second limit per turn.

UI/UX: - A circular table layout showing other players' avatars and card counts.

Drag-and-drop or click-to-select functionality for cards.

Clear "Dhumbal" button that enables only when hand value is ≤ 5.

Scoreboard modal showing round-by-round history.

4. Implementation Steps requested:

First, provide the Backend Socket.io logic to handle room creation, card dealing, and turn-based move validation.

Second, provide the Frontend Game Board component that handles card selection and communicates with the socket.

Third, provide the Game Logic Utility to calculate hand values and validate if a "set" or "sequence" is legal.

Please start by outlining the folder structure and then provide the code for the Backend Server.
